    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d44">

                <pb facs="0054" n="50" xml:id="pg50"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.9315 Manchuria/139: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-08-10T20:00:00+03:00">August 10, 1939—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 10—3:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>437, Following from Military Attaché<anchor n="60" xml:id="d44-f1"/> for War
                    Department: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>It has been learned from no less than three reliable sources that there
                            has been a considerable movement of military supplies and some troops to
                            the Far East. It is believed that these troops are going to the
                            trans-Baikal area and are probably being sent in connection with the
                            recent border disturbances around Lake Buir.<anchor n="61"
                                xml:id="d44-f2"/> This movement has been going on for probably 6
                            weeks or 2 months. Reports of routes of train movements would appear to
                            indicate that the majority of the troops being moved are coming from the
                            Ural military district and probably from the southern and southeastern
                            military districts of European Russia. It is not believed that any
                            considerable number of troops have been moved from the military
                            districts of the western frontier although the troops moved to the east
                            would have formed a general reserve for the western forces in the event
                            of Soviet participation in a major war in Europe. End section for War
                            Department.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Potemkin<anchor n="62" xml:id="d44-f3"/> yesterday admitted to me the
                    continuation of fighting on the Manchurian-Mongolian border. There is up to the
                    present no evidence to indicate that the Soviet Government desires to extend the
                    sphere of conflict. Although in so far as can be ascertained no troops have been
                    moved from the Soviet forces on the western frontier, nevertheless the transfer
                    of troops from European Russia at the present time is significant.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
